WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.610
Bong Joon-ho's "Parasite"

00:00:01.610 --> 00:00:05.750
is a virtually perfect
film on every level.

00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:08.178
On the surface, its technical execution

00:00:08.178 --> 00:00:10.570
is so precise and immaculate

00:00:10.570 --> 00:00:13.261
that it's hard to notice the
film's greatest achievement

00:00:13.261 --> 00:00:15.090
hiding underneath:

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:16.740
the screenplay.

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:20.187
For Bong, who has written every
single film in his career,

00:00:20.187 --> 00:00:22.938
"Parasite" is essentially a culmination

00:00:22.938 --> 00:00:25.680
of everything he's learned over the years.

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:28.580
But in its more than two hours of runtime,

00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:30.221
there is a single moment

00:00:30.221 --> 00:00:33.440
that truly exemplifies his genius,

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:35.970
a sequence that transforms "Parasite"

00:00:35.970 --> 00:00:38.861
into cinematic perfection.

00:00:38.861 --> 00:00:40.519
[doorbell rings]

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:46.667
Like all great stories, "Parasite" has

00:00:46.667 --> 00:00:49.210
a beginning, a middle, and an end,

00:00:49.210 --> 00:00:51.537
yet it never quite follows the usual

00:00:51.537 --> 00:00:54.500
three-act structure we're familiar with.

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:57.860
Instead, the film plays a
lot like two separate movies

00:00:57.860 --> 00:00:59.990
that are joined into one.

00:00:59.990 --> 00:01:02.473
The first film deals
with the two families:

00:01:02.473 --> 00:01:05.100
the impoverished Kims,
who plan to infiltrate

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:08.520
the wealthy Parks by
each posing as a tutor,

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:11.487
a driver, and a housekeeper.

00:01:11.487 --> 00:01:14.240
But it creates an odd moment in the story

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:16.313
about 50 minutes in,

00:01:16.313 --> 00:01:19.450
after the Kims have removed
all of the existing employees

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:22.370
to essentially take over the house.

00:01:22.370 --> 00:01:25.760
Suddenly, there's no conflict
left to carry the film,

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:29.300
and the story comes to a literal stop.

00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:31.208
But it's the sequence that bridges

00:01:31.208 --> 00:01:34.916
the end of the first film
to the unexpected second

00:01:34.916 --> 00:01:37.770
where Bong stages his attack.

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:39.270
Let's take a look.

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:54.920
Bong begins the sequence
by visually establishing

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:57.700
the Kims' false sense of success,

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:00.350
having dedicated an
entire previous sequence

00:02:00.350 --> 00:02:03.800
to show the Kims reaping
the rewards of their scheme.

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:06.217
But he does it most effectively with

00:02:06.217 --> 00:02:09.767
a simple parallel image using a window,

00:02:09.767 --> 00:02:12.120
a motif of luxury that was introduced

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:13.840
earlier in the film.

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:16.830
The Kims, who had previously
been subjected to the views

00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:20.270
of ordinary life outside
their basement apartment,

00:02:20.270 --> 00:02:24.270
discover privacy as a form of luxury.

00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:27.070
Yet, despite all of this, their success

00:02:27.070 --> 00:02:29.920
is only downplayed by their dialogue,

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:34.013
which emphasizes just
how far they are from it.

00:02:45.410 --> 00:02:47.485
Bong keeps the dialogue engaging

00:02:47.485 --> 00:02:50.101
by faking out three moments of tension

00:02:50.101 --> 00:02:52.438
that gradually build over time.

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:57.570
These moments are known
as beats in a dialogue.

00:02:57.570 --> 00:03:01.320
Each beat organically
interrupts and changes the flow

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:03.820
and the topic of conversation.

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:12.140
Until it seemingly
explodes on the third beat.

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:13.724
[glass shatters]

00:03:23.170 --> 00:03:26.287
[both laughing]

00:03:38.294 --> 00:03:39.769
[doorbell rings]

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:42.680
It's no coincidence that this doorbell

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:45.910
marks the exact midpoint
of the screenplay,

00:03:45.910 --> 00:03:50.720
appearing on page 71 out
of a 141-page script.

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:53.730
It's a sound that signifies
the end of the first film

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:57.531
and what Bong refers to as
"the real start of the film."

00:04:11.946 --> 00:04:14.660
It's a brilliantly foreboding moment

00:04:14.660 --> 00:04:17.880
after a series of peaceful sequences.

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:21.390
The audience is aware that
something is about to go wrong,

00:04:21.390 --> 00:04:24.160
they just aren't sure what it is.

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:26.718
This is probably the
best moment to talk about

00:04:26.718 --> 00:04:29.320
the films that inspired "Parasite."

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:31.280
Bong has mentioned several,

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:34.516
and the most obvious is
Kim Ki-young's 1960 film

00:04:34.516 --> 00:04:37.280
"The Housemaid," which
features a similar story

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:40.660
about a poor maid infiltrating the rich.

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:43.940
But thematically, its most
interesting inspiration

00:04:43.940 --> 00:04:47.714
comes from Akira Kurosawa's 1963 film

00:04:47.714 --> 00:04:49.081
"High and Low,"

00:04:49.081 --> 00:04:51.140
one of the first films that used height

00:04:51.140 --> 00:04:54.310
as a visual representation of class,

00:04:54.310 --> 00:04:56.410
with the rich towering above

00:04:56.410 --> 00:04:58.660
and the poor living underneath.

00:04:58.660 --> 00:05:01.130
"Parasite" expands upon this idea

00:05:01.130 --> 00:05:05.113
through another visual
motif introduced earlier:

00:05:05.113 --> 00:05:06.571
stairs.

00:05:06.571 --> 00:05:08.810
On second viewing, it's incredible to see

00:05:08.810 --> 00:05:11.210
how vertical the film is

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:13.710
right from its opening image.

00:05:13.710 --> 00:05:16.210
Whenever a character
climbs a flight of stairs,

00:05:16.210 --> 00:05:19.870
it's a visual symbol of the
rise in the social class,

00:05:19.870 --> 00:05:23.270
while the walk down suggests the opposite.

00:05:23.270 --> 00:05:26.330
Just like the window,
it's this very sequence

00:05:26.330 --> 00:05:28.520
where Bong starts to take advantage

00:05:28.520 --> 00:05:31.710
of all the visual concepts
he set up earlier,

00:05:31.710 --> 00:05:34.490
and it's the reason why we feel so uneasy

00:05:34.490 --> 00:05:36.781
in a moment like this.

00:05:44.830 --> 00:05:47.320
The film that most heavily
inspired "Parasite"

00:05:47.320 --> 00:05:50.430
is probably Alfred Hitchcock's "Psycho."

00:05:50.430 --> 00:05:54.850
The two films share a surprising
number of similarities.

00:05:54.850 --> 00:05:56.440
Both mainly feature a house

00:05:56.440 --> 00:05:59.560
that almost becomes a character itself,

00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:01.330
the architecture guiding the film

00:06:01.330 --> 00:06:04.440
and sometimes hiding
the truth in plain sight

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:06.340
on another level.

00:06:06.340 --> 00:06:09.900
And, most importantly, the
game-changing twist midway

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:14.060
was also done most popularly
by Hitchcock in "Psycho,"

00:06:14.060 --> 00:06:15.328
who killed his main character

00:06:15.328 --> 00:06:18.713
exactly halfway through the film.

00:06:18.713 --> 00:06:21.310
What makes the twist
in "Parasite" so great

00:06:21.310 --> 00:06:23.170
is that it's as predictable

00:06:23.170 --> 00:06:25.930
as it is impossible to see coming.

00:06:25.930 --> 00:06:27.787
The basement in question is featured

00:06:27.787 --> 00:06:29.603
only twice in the film

00:06:29.603 --> 00:06:30.944
for less than a minute

00:06:30.944 --> 00:06:33.870
before its actual role is revealed.

00:06:33.870 --> 00:06:36.850
But it's a twist that
doesn't feel out of question,

00:06:36.850 --> 00:06:39.240
as we've seen it happen already,

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:42.246
just through the eyes of another family.

00:06:44.770 --> 00:06:47.610
The truth finally reveals itself.

00:06:47.610 --> 00:06:49.980
And Bong expertly reveals the twist

00:06:49.980 --> 00:06:52.300
strictly from the Kims' perspective

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:54.368
through a handheld camera.

00:06:55.360 --> 00:06:57.950
As the lighting, camera, tempo,

00:06:57.950 --> 00:07:01.160
and even the genre of the film changes,

00:07:01.160 --> 00:07:03.070
what awaits at the end of the tunnel

00:07:03.070 --> 00:07:06.100
is an entirely different film.

00:07:06.100 --> 00:07:09.113
All in 10 minutes of a sequence.

00:07:09.950 --> 00:07:12.090
What makes "Parasite" so perfect

00:07:12.090 --> 00:07:16.430
is that it understands the
rules and power of storytelling.

00:07:16.430 --> 00:07:19.360
Everything on screen
has a specific purpose

00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:24.070
and a meaning that transforms
the story as it unpacks.

00:07:24.070 --> 00:07:27.050
And it's ironic that, as
brilliant as Bong's plan

00:07:27.050 --> 00:07:30.140
for the story is, the genius of "Parasite"

00:07:30.140 --> 00:07:32.520
lies in the 10-minute sequence

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:36.873
where an entire plan
is demolished on sight.

